0.150.547 To replicate the same behaviour of present claim 1 in Hu, i.e. to make the second "variable" path element twice the length of the first, requires 2(L + 20AL) = L + 22AL. Solving this returns AL = L/2.

In the first interference arm of Hu, each "reference" waveguide has length L and the nth"variable" waveguide has length L +  SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.1470.707 In contrast, in claim 1 of the present patentapplication, the length of the Nth "variable" waveguide is independent of the length of any of the "reference" waveguides. The length of the Nth "variable" waveguide in claim 1 of the present patent application is 2NLp. As noted previously, L, is simply referred to as L in the present patent application, however we refer to it as L, here to avoid confusion with the L used in Hu, which is different and should not be conflated with our L. For clarity, Applicant also notes that Claim 1 of the present patent application defines N as an integer (i.e., the first "variable" waveguide can correspond to N=0), while paragraph [0008] of Hu defines n = 1, 2, ... (i.e. the first "variable" waveguide corresponds to n=1).

To replicate the same behaviour in Hu, i.e. to make the second "variable" path element twice the length of the first, we require 2(L + 20AL) = L + 22AL. Solving this returns AL = L/2. If we now input this value of AL into the formula for the third "variable" path element, we find ithas length L +  SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.1430.827 L + 24(L/2) = L + 23L = 9L. This can be compared to the length ofthe first "variable" path element, L + 20AL = L + L/2 = 3L/2, and the length of the second "variable" path element, L + 22AL = L + 4L/2 = 3L. Combining the first and third "variable" path elements creates a total delay path of 21L/2, which is 7x the length of the first "variable" path element, as opposed to 5x the length of the first "variable" path element in the present patent application.

If one were to use only the second interference arm from Hu in isolation, with a "reference" waveguide that has length substantially greater than zero, which one should not, then one may attempt to replicate the total delay paths from [0067] "in reverse", as described above. This will require additional "variable" path lengths in the second interference arm, as it has already been established that three "variable" path lengths is insufficient to replicate a total delay path that is 5x the length of the shortest "variable" path element. The length of the mth "variable" waveguide in the second interference arm is L - 22m-1AL, where m=1, 2, ..., j/2 (claims 5 and 7 of Hu). For the second shortest "variable" waveguide to be twice the length of the shortest "variable" waveguide,we require AL = L/(2  -  SVG
