0.1730.36 . In the previous example with three "variable" path lengths, thismeans j/2 = 3, returning AL = L/56, as noted above. As j/2 increases, AL - 0 and so the length of the mth "variable" waveguide, L - 22m-1AL - L for all but the shortest few "variable" waveguides. For example, if j/2 = 5, the "variable" waveguides have lengths 3L/7, 6L/7, 27L/28, 111L/112, and 447L/448, where the lengths of the longest two "variable" waveguides are both >99% of the length of the "reference" waveguide. The shortest "variable" waveguide is always 3L/7, so this caps the longest "variable" waveguide at 2.33x the length of the shortest "variable" waveguide.
